## DESCRIPTION
## Trigonometric Functions
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Trigonometry)
## DBchapter(Triangle trigonometry)
## DBsection(Applications of special triangles & right triangles)
## Date(01/01/10)
## Institution(Fort Lewis College)
## Author(Paul Pearson)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Functions Modeling Change')
## AuthorText1('Connally')
## EditionText1('3')
## Section1(6.Tools)
## Problem1(18)
## KEYWORDS('trig')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'contextTrigDegrees.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

$a = random(15, 21);
$c = random(22, 30);

$ansb = Compute("sqrt($c^2 - $a^2)");

$ansA = Compute("arcsin($a/$c)");
$ansB = Compute("arccos($a/$c)");

BEGIN_PGML
Suppose that [` a `], [` b`] and [` c `] are the sides of a right triangle, where side [` a `] is across from angle [` A `], side [` b `] is across from angle [` B `], and side [` c `] is across from the right angle.  If [` a = [$a] `] and [` c = [$c] `], find the missing sides and angles in this right triangle.  All angles should be in degrees (not radians), and  all trig functions entered will be evaluated in degrees (not radians).  You may enter inverse trig functionsusing syntax like [` \arccos(2) `] or [` \cos^{-1}(2) `].

a) Side [` b `] = [_]{$ansb}

    [@  helpLink('numbers')  @]*

b) Angle [` A `] = [_]{$ansA} degrees

    [@  helpLink('angles')  @]*

c) Angle [` B `] = [_]{$ansB} degrees

    [@  helpLink('angles')  @]*
END_PGML

COMMENT("Uses trig functions to be in degrees (instead of the default radians)"
);
ENDDOCUMENT();
